"su.pwr.switch":"One cable from a {poe} PoE‑switch port into the <b>PoE‑in</b> port carries both data and power (up to {watt} for this model). If you are unsure of the port's budget, use 802.3at. Wait 30–60 s for the PWR/SYS LED to go solid.",
"su.pwr.injector":"There is no PoE switch on site. Fit a PoE injector: <em>DATA IN</em> to a plain switch/router port, <em>P+DATA OUT</em> to the AP's <b>PoE‑in</b> port. The injector runs off 230 V mains and must sit in a dry indoor spot. Total copper run: 100 m maximum.",
"su.pwr.dc":"There is a mains socket at the mounting point but no PoE. Plug the <b>{dcv}</b> adapter into the DC jack. The network cable then carries data only.",
"su.note.nodc":"This model has no DC input — power is PoE only (a PoE switch or an injector). The adapter option does not apply to it.",
"su.note.dc2port":"On DC power the second LAN port carries data only — a camera or phone after the AP needs its own power source.",
"su.note.pass2port":"This model has a second, pass‑through LAN port for one in‑room device. It shares the AP's PoE budget and is not a second uplink.",
"su.note.uplink25":"The uplink is 2.5 GbE. To actually pass more than 1 Gbps, plug PoE‑in into a 2.5 GbE (or faster) switch port; a plain 1 GbE port still works, capped at 1 Gbps.",
"su.note.outdoor":"Outdoor run: shielded outdoor‑rated cable, the supplied gland fully seated on the RJ45, a drip loop below the AP, the grounding screw bonded to earth, and an in‑line surge protector where the cable enters the building.",
"su.note.623ewind":"1.07 kg plus wind load — mount it on the supplied steel clamp on a rated pole and aim the directional antennas (10 / 5 dBi) at the coverage area.",
"su.reach.sadp":"Put the laptop on the same subnet. Run the <b>SADP</b> tool — it lists every Hikvision device on the segment. Match the row by the <b>MAC address on the label</b> (not the model name); the Security column shows <em>Inactive</em>.",
"su.reach.dhcp":"Open the site router/gateway page and find the DHCP lease whose <b>MAC matches the AP's label</b> — the address next to it is its IP. Use this when you cannot install SADP.",
"su.reach.direct":"Set the laptop to a static address in the QSG's range (often <code>192.168.1.10 / 255.255.255.0</code>), connect the AP straight to the laptop, and browse to the default IP (often <code>192.168.1.64</code> — confirm in the QSG).",
"su.reach.hikpartner":"Log in to the <b>Hik‑Partner Pro</b> app, open the site, and add the device — it is found on the LAN or by QR code / serial number. The app walks you through activation, Wi‑Fi and adding it to the site; screens below.",
"su.s3.p":"A new unit is inactive. Set the <code>admin</code> password — 8–16 characters using at least two of lowercase / uppercase / digits / symbols — and record it at once. Then set the IP mode (DHCP reservation or a static address outside the pool) and time / NTP.",
"su.s4.p":"One SSID on both bands with band steering on. 2.4 GHz: 20 MHz width, channels 1/6/11. 5 GHz: 80 MHz, auto‑channel with DFS. Transmit power below maximum. Update the firmware before you leave.",
"su.note.wpa3":"This model supports WPA3 — use WPA2/WPA3‑mixed for a general network, pure WPA3 only if every client is modern.",
"su.note.nowpa3":"This model supports WPA2, not WPA3 — use WPA2‑PSK (AES).",
"su.s5.p":"Walk the area with a Wi‑Fi analyser for dead spots, speed‑test near and far, and test roaming between APs. Record the admin password, IPs, SSIDs and passphrases, and PoE port assignments. Transfer the site to the customer's Hik‑Connect.",
